  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "not stated",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper reports held-out perplexity and downstream accuracy across model sizes, but does not fit or test the original scaling-law equations.",
    "gate_note": "The paper cites scaling laws and reports results across parameter scales. It does not fit scaling exponents, test small-run extrapolation, or seek a compute-optimal parameter/token allocation. Rows use Pile perplexity as the primary held-out language-model measure and reported downstream average accuracy; comparisons among architectural variants are not evidence about scaling-law fit.",
    "test_reason": "This is an architecture paper proposing MeSH for recursive transformers. It cites Kaplan and Hoffmann only as motivation in the introduction. It reports perplexity at several Pythia sizes (160M–6.9B), but only to compare its own method with baselines at each size. It fits no power law, tests no extrapolation and studies no allocation. The drop in perplexity with size is incidental, and it is confounded by per-size changes in depth, learning rate and recursive configuration. Its own results therefore do not bear on whether any version holds.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that MeSH improves recursive transformers over recursive baselines, that its gains hold from 160M to 6.9B, and that at 1.4B it beats the larger vanilla model. They draw no conclusion about scaling-law form, extrapolation or allocation.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper trains and evaluates recursive-transformer variants across model sizes; it does not use the original scaling-law fitting or extrapolation procedure.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Perplexity and downstream results vary with recursive architecture and MeSH versus baseline variant; the paper also compares different loop configurations.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Two architecture changes shift the loss-versus-non-embedding-parameter curve. Weight-shared recursion raises loss at matched compute, and routed memory (MeSH) lowers it. The authors report a 1.46x parameter-efficiency gain over vanilla at the 1.4B scale (Fig. 7, Table 1).",
        "proposed_claim": "Architectural changes such as recursion or routed state can shift the offset of loss-versus-parameter curves at matched compute.",
        "row_ids": [
          "p10",
          "p11",
          "p12"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/LivingFutureLab/MeSH/",
    "peer_reviewed": "Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026",
    "problems": "No scaling-law fit, exponent estimates, or extrapolation test. Parameter size changes alongside architecture and training compute; the largest models have no vanilla counterparts. The reported scale range and setup do not test token- or compute-scaling independently.",
    "rows_left_out": "Other reported PPL datasets (Wikitext and both Lambada versions), other recursive schemes/configurations, and downstream accuracy results are not included; the retained rows prioritize Pile held-out perplexity as the primary language-model loss measure for V1.",
    "untested": [],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "The reader's 'tests'/T2 and its V1 'supports' label are overturned. The perplexity decline with size is a by-product of the Pythia size ladder, which also varies depth, width and LR. The paper makes no power-law, exponent or extrapolation claim. Figure 8 varies layer distribution at matched compute but not at fixed parameter count, so it does not bear on V4. Downstream accuracy across sizes is likewise unfitted, so it does not bear on V6."
  },
